---
import SectionHeading from '../SectionHeading.astro';
import SubHeading from '../logo/SubHeading.astro';
import ColorCards from '../color/ColorCards';
import RecipeBar from '../color/RecipeBar.astro';
import Pairings from '../color/Pairings.astro';
import { BRAND_COLORS } from '../../../brand/palette';
---

<section id="color" class="scroll-mt-28 py-16">
  <SectionHeading
    eyebrow="Color"
    title="Color"
    lead={`These ${BRAND_COLORS.length} colors are the palette. Click any value to copy it. In interfaces, use the token rather than the hex value.`}
  />

  <ColorCards client:visible />

  <p
    class="m-0 mt-4 max-w-[68ch] text-[length:var(--type-callout-size)] leading-[var(--type-callout-line)] text-[var(--label-secondary)]"
  >
    Contrast is shown with white and with Ink as a ratio, then graded: AA means any text, AA Large
    means text 18pt or larger and graphics only, Fail means neither. The web palette is the standard
    everywhere: web, Office, Google, print, the studio and the Claude skill.
  </p>

  <SubHeading
    id="color-recipe"
    title="How much of each"
    lead="The standard mix for every page, slide and post: White and the neutrals for most of it, the teals for brand surfaces and things you can act on, and a little Magenta and Yellow."
  />
  <RecipeBar />

  <SubHeading
    id="color-pairings"
    title="Pairings that work"
    lead="Each card is a background. The words on it show which colors can sit on top and how big the text needs to be, worked out from the contrast ratio of each pair. Contrast is the floor: magenta still only means something is wrong."
  />
  <Pairings />
</section>
